# The StreamDB v3 reader over vendor/streamdb-v3/corpus.streamdb -- the
# 24-document container the REAL upstream C writer produced (PROVENANCE.md
# has the recipe, the digests and the upstream commit).
#
# stdout is examples/streamdb/probatio.exsc's certificate stream, 32,591
# bytes, compared with `cmp` against expected.out. Its sections, and what a
# differing byte means:
#
#   0..4      the open: verdict 0, then commit sequence 3
#   5..16     24 documents, 373 trie nodes, winning header slot 128
#   17..      per key of the frozen corpus IN GEN.PY'S ORDER: the verdict,
#             the payload length, and THE PAYLOAD ITSELF -- so a byte that
#             differs here names the key it belongs to by position, and the
#             comparison is against vendor/streamdb-v3/payloads/ byte for
#             byte, not against a hash of them
#   tail      `.t3dm` then `.bin`: the match count, then each match's size
#             and crc32 IN THE READER'S OWN TRAVERSAL ORDER -- the order
#             vendor/streamdb-v3/expectation.json captured from the embedded
#             reference reader, not a predicted one
#
# expected.out is built by expecta.py in this directory, which is an
# INDEPENDENT Python reading of the format and asserts itself against
# expectation.json -- every payload against payloads/, every crc32 and size
# against the manifest's documents array, both suffix orders against the
# manifest's suffix_search section -- before it writes a byte. It is
# verification-only and tests/run.sh never calls it.
#
# The three negative containers are streamdb_onus/ (one flipped payload
# byte), streamdb_caput/ (a flipped header byte, and the fallback to the
# older commit) and streamdb_truncus/ (300 bytes, no valid commit).
#
# 0.044 s wall, three runs, on the tree this landed in: 80,300 syscalls at
# one byte each, and no buffered read (probatio.exsc says why).
TEST: expect-exit=0 stdin=vendor/streamdb-v3/corpus.streamdb sources=examples/streamdb/lector_streamdb.exsc,examples/streamdb/probatio.exsc
